{{Quote|What the hell is Khofar?''"<br />"''A relaxing forest retreat in the Outer Rim where the Wookiee Jedi lives.|[[Mae-ho Aniseya]] and [[The Stranger|Qimir]]|<ref name="Revenge Justice" />}}
'''Khofar'''<ref name="Revenge Justice" /> was a [[planet]]<ref name="Teach / Corrupt">{{Acolyte|Teach / Corrupt}}</ref> in the [[Outer Rim Territories]] that featured [[forest]]<ref name="Revenge Justice" /> and [[jungle]] terrain. During the [[High Republic Era]], the [[Wookiee]] [[Jedi Master]] [[Kelnacca]] lived a solitary life in the jungles of Khofar.<ref name="Character Posters" />

{{Planet-stub}}

==Behind the scenes==
===Milestones===
Khofar first appeared in "[[Revenge / Justice]],"<ref name="Revenge Justice" /> the second episode of the [[Disney+]] series ''[[Star Wars: The Acolyte]]'', released on [[June 4]], [[2024]].<ref name="R/J Episode Guide">{{SW|url=series/the-acolyte/revenge-justice-episode-guide|text="Revenge / Justice" Episode Guide &#124; The Acolyte}}</ref> The planet was first depicted in a trailer shown exclusively at [[Celebration Europe (2023)|Celebration Europe]]<ref name="Celebration">{{CelebrationTrailer|''[[Star Wars: The Acolyte]]''|Celebration Europe (2023)}}</ref> during the [[Lucasfilm]] Studio Showcase on [[April 7]], [[2023]].<ref name="Studio Showcase">{{SW|url=news/swce-2023-lucasfilm-studio-showcase|text=SWCE 2023: 11 Highlights from Lucasfilm's Studio Showcase}}</ref> Khofar was later identified on a character poster for Kelnacca originally posted on [[StarWars.com]] on [[May 28]], [[2024]].<ref name="Character Posters" /> In early versions of the story, Kelnacca went into hiding on the Wookiee [[homeworld]] of [[Kashyyyk]], but Khofar was later created to explore new locations.<ref name="Day Trivia Gallery S2">{{SW|url=day-trivia-gallery-the-acolyte|text="Day" Trivia Gallery &#124; The Acolyte}} {{C|Slide 2}}</ref>

===Filming and set design===
{{Quote|I ended up taking over a soundstage and we built a massive forest that allowed five weeks' worth of shooting full of fauna and maybe 160 real trees, seven made trees, a full 360 painted backing so you couldn't shoot off the set.|Kevin Jenkins|<ref name="Ars Technica">{{WebCite|url=https://arstechnica.com/culture/2024/06/star-wars-behind-the-scenes-creating-the-unique-aesthetic-of-the-acolyte|text=Star Wars behind the scenes: Creating the unique aesthetic of ''The Acolyte''|author=Jennifer Ouellette|date=2024-06-26|work=[[Wikipedia:Ars Technica|''Ars Technica'']]|archivedate=20240704230817}}</ref>}}
As a jungle was required for filming of scenes set<ref name="Ars Technica" /> on Khofar,<ref name="Day" /> ''The Acolyte''{{'s}} crew, including production designer [[Kevin Jenkins]], chose the [[Wikipedia:Portugal|Portuguese]] island of [[Wikipedia:Madeira|Madeira]], which was subsequently also utilized for various other locations in the series. Madeira served as the filming location for exterior shots of the jungle,<ref name="Ars Technica" /> the bridge area<ref name="T/C Trivia Gallery">{{SW|url=teach-corrupt-trivia-gallery-the-acolyte|text="Teach / Corrupt" Trivia Gallery &#124; The Acolyte}} {{C|Slide 2}}</ref> where Mae and "Qimir" land in the episode "[[Day (episode)|Day]],"<ref name="Day" /> and the hillside<ref name="Twitter 1">{{Twitter|username=kev_jenkins|url=status/1805897402819223837|name=[[Kevin Jenkins]]|date=2024-06-26|quote=Round of applause for Alex Garcia Lopez, Director of Episodes 4 and 5 of #TheAcolyte, prepping a shot on location! Great taste in wine and delivered two incredibly tough episodes !! Bravo #StarWars #theacolyte #jedi #sith #maderia|archiveurl=https://archive.ph/gHaMg}}</ref> where Bazil first leads the Jedi in the same episode.<ref name="Day" /> The bridge at the landing site area, which Jenkins described as difficult to reach, was an existing structure with set decoration.<ref name="Twitter 2">{{Twitter|username=kev_jenkins|url=status/1804120699214205045|name=[[Kevin Jenkins]]|date=2024-06-21|quote=Tough Location to find and get down to as it was in a Valley off the beaten track. The Landing site was at the base of a valley in beautiful #maderia. Set dec working on the bridge. #StarWars #TheAcolyte|archiveurl=https://archive.ph/ZF53I}}</ref> Production in Madeira took place between [[March 12]] through [[March 28]], [[2023]].<ref name="JM">{{WebCite|url=https://www.jm-madeira.pt/regiao/chegou_ao_fim_a_aventura_da_star_wars_na_madeira-XGJMART202662|text=Chegou ao fim a aventura da 'Star Wars' na Madeira|author=Carla Sousa|date=2023-03-29|work=[https://www.jm-madeira.pt/ JM]|language=[[Wikipedia:European Portuguese|European Portuguese]]|archivedate=20240531161639}}</ref>

As Madeira was only suitable for exterior shots of the jungle, Jenkins, inspired by the construction of a forest set for [[Dagobah]]<ref name="Ars Technica" /> in the [[1980]] [[original trilogy]] film [[Star Wars: Episode V The Empire Strikes Back|''Star Wars'': Episode V ''The Empire Strikes Back'']],<ref name="ESB">{{Film|V}}</ref> wished to build a similar set for ''The Acolyte''.<ref name="Ars Technica" /> The decision to use practical sets was also led by showrunner [[Leslye Headland]], who did not wish to use [[Wikipedia:StageCraft|The Volume]], the soundstage featuring virtual landscapes utilized in other ''[[Star Wars]]'' productions.<ref name="GamesRadar">{{WebCite|url=https://www.gamesradar.com/entertainment/sci-fi-shows/the-acolyte-sets-interview-amandla-stenberg-forest-map|text=New Star Wars show The Acolyte's sets were so big and detailed, the actors needed a map to get around and were literally "tripping on mushrooms"|author=Bradley Russell|date=2024-06-03|work=[[Wikipedia:GamesRadar+|GamesRadar+]]|archivedate=20240622105325}}</ref> The production crew constructed an extensive forest on a soundstage<ref name="Ars Technica" /> in a warehouse,<ref name="GamesRadar" /> featuring approximately 160 real trees, seven artificial trees, and a full painted backing. The set also contained numerous other plants, fauna, a misted ceiling, and running water. The design of Khofar's trees, which Jenkins compared to spiders' webs or feet,<ref name="Ars Technica" /> was influenced by several paintings made by [[Ralph McQuarrie]], including one<ref name="Day Trivia Gallery S3">{{SW|url=day-trivia-gallery-the-acolyte|text="Day" Trivia Gallery &#124; The Acolyte}} {{C|Slide 3}}</ref> originally created for Dagobah in ''The Empire Strikes Back''.<ref name="McQuarrie">''[[Star Wars Art: Ralph McQuarrie]]''</ref>

Five weeks' worth of shooting took place in the Khofar set.<ref name="Ars Technica" /> Yord Fandar actor [[Charlie Barnett]] described the Khofar set as an entire ecosystem that even had birds living in the ceiling.<ref name="GamesRadar" /> Due to the confusing nature of the set, a map was created and provided to arrivals on set.<ref name="Ars Technica" /> [[Amandla Stenberg]], who portrayed Mae<ref name="GamesRadar" /> and Osha,<ref name="Day" /> recalled noticing the map and asking whether it was intended to be a [[canon]] map before being informed it was real. [[Jecki Lon]] actor [[Dafne Keen]] commented that she had literally tripped on mushrooms while on set.<ref name="GamesRadar" /> For "Day,"<ref name="Day" /> the dormant umbramoths wrapped around the trees were largely practical on-set; the umbramoth that moves was created using a hollow tree trunk with a performer inside.<ref name="Julian Foddy">{{SW|url=news/ilm-the-acolyte-vfx-interview|text=Lightsabers & Magic: Visual Effects Supervisor Julian Foddy on <i>The Acolyte</i>'s Creatures and Weapons of the Jedi}}</ref>

The sequence of events on Khofar in "Day"<ref name="Day" /> with the time transitioning from afternoon through to sunset and then into night was intended to parallel the heightening tension of the episode as an indicator that a dangerous moment was approaching. Cinematographer [[Chris Teague]] described finding the right level of darkness for night scenes without detracting from the viewing experience to be challenging, wishing to ensure that the set design, as well as the action, was visible. Practical effects were used, with the setting sun in the background being constructed with a circle of plywood wrapped in gold fabric, which was hit with light to create a blood-red glow of the sunset. For the scene where Mae finds Kelnacca's body and the sun completely sets, Tungsten Fresnel lights on cranes were dropped at the correct moment to further amplify the tension.<ref name="Ars Technica" />

